// The section "Decorators" of the English guide; guide.rk lists the sections.

import { Example } from "../../example.rk"

export comp Decorators() {
    return <>
        <p>
            A decorator adds logic to components: state, functions and <code>mount()</code>. It is
            declared with <code>dec</code> and applied with <code>@name(arguments)</code> before
            {" "}<code>comp</code>. Like a component, it exists only at compile time: its code is
            inlined into every component it is applied to, and each of them gets its own state.
        </p>
        <Example name="decorators" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                The component sees only what is <b>public</b>: it reads <code>public state</code>,
                {" "}<code>public derived</code> and <code>public const</code> as
                {" "}<code>@ticker.ticks</code> and calls a <code>public func</code> as
                {" "}<code>@ticker.toggle()</code>. In markup, <code>@ticker.ticks</code> updates
                like the component's own state. Other names of the
                decorator are its own and cannot clash with the component's.
            </li>
            <li>
                Outside the decorator its members are <b>read-only</b>:
                {" "}<code>@ticker.ticks = 0</code> is an error, changes go through its public
                functions. There is no <code>public let</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
                The arguments are positional, as in a call, and the parameters may have default
                values: <code>@ticker</code> ticks every second. The arguments see the properties
                of the component, <code>@ticker(label.length * 100)</code>, and are computed again
                when they change.
            </li>
            <li>
                With several decorators, <code>@a @b comp X</code>, their bodies run from top to
                bottom before the component's, and <code>mount()</code> runs from the inside out:
                the component, <code>b</code>, <code>a</code>. <code>@html-tag</code> goes first.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A decorator can also <b>wrap</b> what the component returns: its body ends with
            {" "}<code>{"return (content Element) => ..."}</code>, which gets the component's markup
            and gives other markup instead. A decorator that <code>needs</code> another one sees its
            public members.
        </p>
        <Example name="decorator-wrappers" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                The type of the wrapper's parameter is written: it limits where the decorator can
                be applied, so <code>{"(form HTMLFormElement) => ..."}</code> fits only components
                that return a form. <code>{"<Product />"}</code> has the type of what the outermost
                wrapper returns.
            </li>
            <li>
                The wrapper runs once, when the component is created. Its markup reads the
                decorator's state and updates like the component's own. The wrappers apply from
                the bottom decorator up.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>dec quantityBadge() needs quantity</code> may use
                {" "}<code>@quantity.count</code> even in its body, so <code>@quantity</code>
                {" "}must be applied above it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <code>export dec</code> exports a decorator, and another module imports it by its
                name: <code>{'import { quantity } from "./quantity.rk"'}</code>. Its code is
                inlined there, so it cannot assign the variables of its own module, and the
                decorators it needs are exported too.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            A decorator can <b>include</b> others:
            {" "}<code>dec card(title string) includes framed(title), likes</code> applies them
            with it, above its own body, and their arguments see its parameters. The component
            reads all their public members through it, <code>@card.count</code>. A
            {" "}<code>public ref</code> gives the component an element of the wrapper.
        </p>
        <Example name="decorator-includes" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <code>@card</code> counts as <code>@likes</code> applied: it satisfies
                {" "}<code>needs likes</code> of a decorator below it, and <code>@likes</code> next
                to it is applied twice. Two members with the same name are an error.
            </li>
            <li>
                A ref always points to its element: <code>@card.box = x</code> is an error, but
                the element itself can be changed, <code>@card.box.style.color = "red"</code>.
            </li>
            <li>Not yet: an exported decorator that includes others.</li>
        </ul>
    </>
}
